### Create an Item

Temporal values live in `properties` only, as RFC 3339 with an offset. Either
`datetime` is set, or it is `null` and both `start_datetime` and `end_datetime` are given.

```elixir
item_data = %{
  "id" => "my-test-item",
  "collection_id" => "my-test-collection",
  "stac_version" => "1.0.0",
  "geometry" => %{"type" => "Point", "coordinates" => [26.72, 58.38]},
  "bbox" => [26.72, 58.38, 26.72, 58.38],
  "properties" => %{"datetime" => "2024-05-01T21:27:48+03:00", "title" => "Tartu"},
  "assets" => %{
    "data" => %{
      "href" => "https://example.com/data/tartu.tif",
      "type" => "image/tiff; application=geotiff",
      "roles" => ["data"]
    }
  }
}

{:ok, response} = Req.post(manage_req, url: "/items", json: item_data)

IO.puts("Status: #{response.status}")
# +03:00 is normalised to UTC; created/updated sit inside properties for items
response.body["data"]["properties"]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
Status: 201
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{
  "created" => "2026-11-16T15:57:52.973965Z",
  "datetime" => "2024-05-01T18:27:48Z",
  "title" => "Tartu",
  "updated" => "2026-11-16T15:57:52.973965Z"
}
```

A datetime without an offset is refused with `400`; nothing is silently coerced.

```elixir
naive_item = %{item_data | "id" => "my-naive-item", "properties" => %{"datetime" => "2024-05-01T18:27:48"}}

{:ok, response} = Req.post(manage_req, url: "/items", json: naive_item)

IO.puts("Status: #{response.status}")
response.body
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
Status: 400
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{"error" => "properties.datetime must carry a UTC offset, e.g. \"2024-05-01T18:27:48Z\""}
```

## Example 3: Updating Resources (With Auth)

### Full Update (PUT)

```elixir
updated_catalog_data = %{
  "id" => "my-test-catalog",
  "title" => "My Updated Test Catalog",
  "description" => "This catalog has been updated with new information",
  "type" => "Catalog",
  "stac_version" => "1.0.0"
}

{:ok, response} =
  Req.put(manage_req, url: "/catalogs/my-test-catalog", json: updated_catalog_data)

IO.puts("Status: #{response.status}")
response.body["data"] |> Map.take(["id", "title", "description", "created", "updated"])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
Status: 200
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{
  "created" => "2026-11-16T15:57:52.911070Z",
  "description" => "This catalog has been updated with new information",
  "id" => "my-test-catalog",
  "title" => "My Updated Test Catalog",
  "updated" => "2026-11-16T15:57:53.043396Z"
}
```

### Partial Update (PATCH)

```elixir
# Only change the title, keep everything else
{:ok, response} =
  Req.patch(manage_req,
    url: "/catalogs/my-test-catalog",
    json: %{"id" => "my-test-catalog", "title" => "Partially Updated Catalog"}
  )

IO.puts("Status: #{response.status}")
response.body["data"] |> Map.take(["id", "title", "description", "updated"])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
Status: 200
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{
  "description" => "This catalog has been updated with new information",
  "id" => "my-test-catalog",
  "title" => "Partially Updated Catalog",
  "updated" => "2026-11-16T15:57:53.062003Z"
}
```

## Example 4: Deleting Resources (With Auth)

⚠️ **Warning**: deleting a catalog CASCADE deletes all its children (nested catalogs,
collections, items and their assets)!

### Delete a Specific Catalog

```elixir
# Delete the nested catalog first
{:ok, response} = Req.delete(manage_req, url: "/catalogs/my-nested-catalog")

IO.puts("Status: #{response.status}")
response.body
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
Status: 200
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{
  "cascade_deleted" => %{"catalogs" => 0, "collections" => 0, "items" => 0},
  "message" => "Catalog deleted successfully",
  "success" => true
}
```

### Delete with Cascade

```elixir
# Deletes the catalog AND everything below it (here: the collection and its item)
{:ok, response} = Req.delete(manage_req, url: "/catalogs/my-test-catalog")

IO.puts("Status: #{response.status}")
response.body["cascade_deleted"]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
Status: 404
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
nil
```

## Example 5: Personal API Keys

Besides the two static keys, the admin (the static `STAC_API_KEY`) can issue personal
keys, eg. one per colleague, student or script. Each one can be renamed, given an expiry
date, rotated or deleted on its own, without touching the keys everyone else uses.

|                                                     | static `STAC_API_KEY` | issued `read_write` | issued `read_only` / static RO |
| --------------------------------------------------- | --------------------- | ------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| read private catalogs (`/stac/api/v1`, web browser) | ✓                   | ✓                 | ✓                            |
| write catalogs, collections, items                  | ✓                   | ✓                 | 401                            |
| manage keys (`/stac/manage/v1/api-keys`)            | ✓                   | 401                 | 401                            |

A few rules:

* The **role** (`read_only` or `read_write`) is fixed when the key is created. The secret
  starts with `stac_ro_` or `stac_rw_`, so `key_prefix` always shows the role.
* Only a hash is stored. The secret is returned **once**, under `data.key`, on create and
  rotate. Store it right away; if it is lost, rotate.
* `expires_at` is optional. An expired key is treated as no key at all: anonymous on the
  public API, 401 on the Management API, and it does not unlock the web browser.
* `contact` and `note` are free text, so in a year it is still clear whose key it is.

The cells below use `manage_req` (the static key) as the admin client. To keep secrets out
of the notebook output, they print only the `key_prefix`.

```elixir
# Only show the first characters of a secret
mask = fn key -> String.slice(key, 0, 12) <> "…" end

# A client for each API, authenticated with any key
stac_client = fn key -> Req.new(base_url: base_url <> api_version, headers: [{"x-api-key", key}]) end
manage_client = fn key -> Req.new(base_url: base_url <> manage_version, headers: [{"x-api-key", key}]) end

# Unique suffix, so the demo can run more than once
suffix = System.unique_integer([:positive])
:ok
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:ok
```

### A private catalog to try the keys on

Content in a private catalog is hidden from anonymous requests, which makes it easy to see
what a key unlocks.

```elixir
demo_catalog = "keys-demo-#{suffix}"
demo_collection = "keys-demo-collection-#{suffix}"

{:ok, %{status: 201}} =
  Req.post(manage_req,
    url: "/catalogs",
    json: %{
      "id" => demo_catalog,
      "title" => "API key demo (private)",
      "description" => "Private catalog for the API key examples",
      "private" => true
    }
  )

{:ok, %{status: 201}} =
  Req.post(manage_req,
    url: "/collections",
    json: %{
      "id" => demo_collection,
      "title" => "API key demo collection",
      "description" => "Lives in a private catalog",
      "license" => "CC-BY-4.0",
      "catalog_id" => demo_catalog
    }
  )

{:ok, %{status: 201}} =
  Req.post(manage_req,
    url: "/items",
    json: %{
      "id" => "keys-demo-item-#{suffix}",
      "collection_id" => demo_collection,
      "geometry" => %{"type" => "Point", "coordinates" => [26.72, 58.38]},
      "bbox" => [26.72, 58.38, 26.72, 58.38],
      "properties" => %{"datetime" => "2024-05-01T18:27:48Z"}
    }
  )

{:ok, anonymous} = Req.get(req, url: "/collections/#{demo_collection}")
IO.puts("Anonymous GET /collections/#{demo_collection}: #{anonymous.status}")
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
Anonymous GET /collections/keys-demo-collection-14022: 404
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:ok
```

### Issue a read-only key

`role` defaults to `read_only`. `contact` and `note` are optional.

```elixir
{:ok, response} =
  Req.post(manage_req,
    url: "/api-keys",
    json: %{
      "name" => "Alex ro demo - field data #{suffix}",
      "contact" => "alexander.kmoch@ut.ee",
      "note" => "Reads the private soil collections from QGIS"
    }
  )

IO.puts("Status: #{response.status}")
IO.puts(response.body["message"])

# Keep the secret in a variable; it cannot be fetched again
ro_key = response.body["data"]["key"]
ro_key_id = response.body["data"]["id"]

Map.update!(response.body["data"], "key", mask)
```

